{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments ["<b>And who did not lie in wait.</b> He did not ambush him and did not intend it." "<b>Lie in wait.</b> A term of ambush — and so it says ״וְאַתָּה צֹדֶה אֶת נַפְשִׁי״ (I Samuel 24). And it is not correct to say צָדָה in the sense of ״הַצָּד צַיִד״ (Genesis 27), for in hunting animals the ה (heh) does not fall in its verb, and its noun is צַיִד, whereas this one's noun is צְדִיָּה, and its verb is צוֹדֶה, and this verb of his is צָד. And I say its solution is as its Targum renders — וּדְלָא כְמַן לֵהּ. And Menachem classified it under the root צָד צַיִד, and I do not agree with him; and if his colleague has [a form] in one of the categories of צָד, let us classify it under the category of ״עַל צַד תִּנָּשֵׂאוּ״ (Isaiah 66), ״צִדָּה אוֹרֶה״ (I Samuel 20), ״וּמִלִּין לְצַד עִלָּאָה יְמַלֵּל״ (Daniel 7) — so too here, אֲשֶׁר לֹא צָדָה — he did not scheme to find for him any pretext for execution; but even this one can be questioned — in any case its meaning is one who lies in ambush." "<b>And the Elohim brought it about for his hand.</b> He brought it to his hand — as in ״לֹא תְאֻנֶּה אֵלֶיךָ רָעָה״ (Psalms 91), ״לֹא יְאֻנֶּה לַצַּדִּיק כָּל אָוֶן״ (Proverbs 12), ״מִתְאַנֶּה הוּא לִי״ (II Kings 5) — 'he keeps happening upon a pretext against me.'" "<b>And the Elohim brought it about for his hand.</b> And why should this go forth from before Him? It is what David said: ״כַּאֲשֶׁר יֹאמַר מְשַׁל הַקַּדְמֹנִי מֵרְשָׁעִים יֵצֵא רֶשַׁע״ (I Samuel 24). And the proverb of the Kadmoni is the Torah, for it is the proverb of the Holy One, blessed be He, who is the First (קַדְמוֹן) of the world. And where does the Torah say 'from the wicked one goes forth wickedness'? 'And the Elohim brought it about for his hand.' What is Scripture speaking of? Of two men, one killed unintentionally and one killed intentionally, and there were no witnesses to testify. This one was not executed, and that one did not go into exile. The Holy One, blessed be He, arranged them at one inn. The one who killed intentionally sits beneath the ladder, and the one who killed unintentionally climbs the ladder and falls upon the one who killed intentionally and kills him, and witnesses testify against him and obligate him to go into exile. Thus this one who killed unintentionally goes into exile, and that one who killed intentionally is killed (Makkot 10a)." "<b>And I will assign you a place.</b> Even in the wilderness — that he may flee there. And which place protects him? This is the camp of Leviyyah (Mekhilta)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {1 "The Aramaic phrase וּדְלָא כְמַן לֵהּ appears to be Rashi's own rendering of the Targum's sense; left untranslated as it stands in the Hebrew text. The reference to Menachem's 'colleague' (חברו) likely means Dunash ibn Labrat, Rashi's frequent foil in grammatical disputes."}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:53:42.493746403Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}